C = [
("h1", "7. The Cup and the Day"),
("p", "Claim: the oracles against the nations are tied together by a measure and by a day. The measure is that each nation’s sentence is cut to its ground: what it did to others is done to it, and the conqueror is conquered. The day is the one the prophets call the day of the LORD, on which the cup of Jeremiah 25 passes to every nation and the LORD pleads with all flesh. Scripture reports the first stroke of the cup as begun in Jeremiah’s own time and holds the rest open. The volume reads the passages in order, keeps Babylon and Edom where their own volumes read them, and says plainly what it does not take up."),
("sub", "7.1 Babylon drinks last, and drinks"),
("p", "Jeremiah 25 does not end the list at Egypt or at Tyre. The cup goes to “all the kingdoms of the world, which are upon the face of the earth: and the king of Sheshach shall drink after them” (Jeremiah 25:26). The one who administers the cup is itself on the list. The LORD calls Nebuchadrezzar “my servant” (Jeremiah 25:9) and sends him with the families of the north against Judah and “against all these nations round about,” and then sets his term: “and these nations shall serve the king of Babylon seventy years” (Jeremiah 25:11). Then: “And it shall come to pass, when seventy years are accomplished, that I will punish the king of Babylon, and that nation, saith the LORD, for their iniquity, and the land of the Chaldeans, and will make it perpetual desolations” (Jeremiah 25:12). The verse after gives the book itself a part: “And I will bring upon that land all my words which I have pronounced against it, even all that is written in this book, which Jeremiah hath prophesied against all the nations” (Jeremiah 25:13)."),
("p", "The reason follows, and it is the measure in one sentence: “For many nations and great kings shall serve themselves of them also: and I will recompense them according to their deeds, and according to the works of their own hands” (Jeremiah 25:14). Isaiah’s heading for Babylon is “The burden of Babylon, which Isaiah the son of Amoz did see” (Isaiah 13:1), and Habakkuk’s taunt says the same of the conqueror: “Because thou hast spoiled many nations, all the remnant of the people shall spoil thee” (Habakkuk 2:8). The volume does not take up Babylon. Prophetic Commentary Volume 3 reads Babylon with Revelation 17 and 18, and what it concludes there is cited here and not repeated. This chapter keeps one point from Babylon: the instrument is in the cup. That is why the volume says the oracles are fitted to their grounds. Babylon is not exempt because the LORD used it."),
("sub", "7.2 From the city called by my name to all flesh"),
("p", "The cup is passed in order, and the order tells the reader where the sword begins. “For, lo, I begin to bring evil on the city which is called by my name, and should ye be utterly unpunished? Ye shall not be unpunished: for I will call for a sword upon all the inhabitants of the earth, saith the LORD of hosts” (Jeremiah 25:29). The sentence on the city that bears the LORD’s name is the first drink, and the widening follows. “The LORD shall roar from on high, and utter his voice from his holy habitation; he shall mightily roar upon his habitation; he shall give a shout, as they that tread the grapes, against all the inhabitants of the earth” (Jeremiah 25:30). “A noise shall come even to the ends of the earth; for the LORD hath a controversy with the nations, he will plead with all flesh; he will give them that are wicked to the sword, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 25:31)."),
("p", "The next two verses carry the oracle past anything the book reports. “Thus saith the LORD of hosts, Behold, evil shall go forth from nation to nation, and a great whirlwind shall be raised up from the coasts of the earth” (Jeremiah 25:32), and, “And the slain of the LORD shall be at that day from one end of the earth even unto the other end of the earth: they shall not be lamented, neither gathered, nor buried; they shall be dung upon the ground” (Jeremiah 25:33). The phrase “at that day” gives the sweep a day, and the words “from one end of the earth even unto the other end of the earth” give it a scope. The volume reads the scope as wider than any single campaign the Bible names. The campaigns of Nebuchadrezzar are the first drink, and they are reported as such. The roar of verse 30 and the slain of verse 33 are not reported by any later narrative, and the volume holds them as open."),
("p", "Peels draws the same line from the structure of the collection. He says that in these oracles “the function and meaning of these texts are to be found in the proclamation of the sovereign reign of the King of Zion,” and that “the oracles unanimously put the acts of God at the centre” (//European Journal of Theology// 16:2, 2007). The volume agrees. Where it differs is in what it does with the open clauses. Peels’s article is about the function of the oracles and not about their fulfilment, and the volume’s question is exactly that."),
("sub", "7.3 Obadiah 15 and the cup in the LORD’s hand"),
("p", "Obadiah has been in the volume since chapter 2 as Edom’s accuser. Its centre is verse 15, a sentence that is addressed to Edom and widens in the same verse. “For the day of the LORD is near upon all the heathen: as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee: thy reward shall return upon thine own head” (Obadiah 15). The clause “as thou hast done” is the measure again, put as a rule. Verse 16 takes up the cup: “For as ye have drunk upon my holy mountain, so shall all the heathen drink continually, yea, they shall drink, and they shall swallow down, and they shall be as though they had not been.” The drinking upon the holy mountain is Edom’s and the nations’; the drinking that returns is that of all the heathen. The same figure, a cup to be drunk, runs from Jeremiah 25 to Obadiah, and Habakkuk 2:16 has it at the close of his taunt: “the cup of the LORD’s right hand shall be turned unto thee.”"),
("p", "Obadiah does not stop at the sentence. “But upon mount Zion shall be deliverance, and there shall be holiness; and the house of Jacob shall possess their possessions” (Obadiah 17). “And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s” (Obadiah 21). The last sentence of the book gives the end of the day: a kingdom that is the LORD’s, with Zion as its seat. The fate of Edom itself is read in Prophetic Commentary Volume 5, and the volume cites that reading and does not restate it. What this chapter takes from Obadiah is the shape: the day of the LORD on all the heathen, a measure that returns on the head, and deliverance and a kingdom on Zion."),
("sub", "7.4 Zephaniah, Joel and the gathering of the nations"),
("p", "Zephaniah gives the day as a purpose. “Therefore wait ye upon me, saith the LORD, until the day that I rise up to the prey: for my determination is to gather the nations, that I may assemble the kingdoms, to pour upon them mine indignation, even all my fierce anger: for all the earth shall be devoured with the fire of my jealousy” (Zephaniah 3:8). The word “determination” is the LORD’s own, and the content is a gathering. The next verse turns: “For then will I turn to the people a pure language, that they may all call upon the name of the LORD, to serve him with one consent” (Zephaniah 3:9). The gathering is for judgment and the judgment is followed by a purified speech. Chapter 8 reads the second half."),
("p", "Joel places the gathering in a time: “For, behold, in those days, and in that time, when I shall bring again the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem, I will also gather all nations, and will bring them down into the valley of Jehoshaphat, and will plead with them there for my people and for my heritage Israel, whom they have scattered among the nations, and parted my land” (Joel 3:1, 2). The time is the time when Judah’s captivity is brought again. The cause is what the nations did to Israel: scattered her among the nations and parted her land. The gathering of the nations is therefore tied to the restoration of Judah and Jerusalem, and it is the same clause the volume has met in Jeremiah 48:47, 49:6 and 49:39, the captivity brought again, now attached to a day on which the nations are judged."),
("sub", "7.5 The measure: ground and sentence"),
("p", "The reader can set the grounds of chapter 2 beside the sentences of chapters 3 to 6 and see that the sentences are cut to the grounds. Moab burned the bones of Edom’s king, and fire is sent on Moab (Amos 2:1, 2). Ammon ripped up the women of Gilead to enlarge its border, and Ammon’s border is given away (Ezekiel 25:4, 10). Gaza and Tyre sold captives, and the LORD says to Tyre and Zidon that he will sell their sons and daughters into the hand of Judah (Joel 3:8). Edom stood on the other side, and “as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee” (Obadiah 15). The conqueror who “hast spoiled many nations” is spoiled (Habakkuk 2:8). Egypt was “a staff of reed to the house of Israel” and is made the basest of kingdoms (Ezekiel 29:6, 15). Pharaoh said, “My river is mine own,” and his river is made dry (Ezekiel 29:3; 30:12). The pattern is consistent enough that the volume treats it as a rule of the oracles, not as a coincidence."),
("ctbl", "The measure: what each nation did, and what the LORD says he will do.", [
    ("Nation", "Ground", "Sentence cut to it"),
    ("Moab", "Burned the bones of the king of Edom into lime (Amos 2:1)", "Fire sent upon Moab (Amos 2:2)"),
    ("Ammon", "Enlarged its border by tearing Gilead (Amos 1:13)", "Its land given to the men of the east; Israel heir (Ezekiel 25:4, 10; Jeremiah 49:2)"),
    ("Tyre and Zidon", "Sold the children of Judah to the Grecians (Joel 3:6)", "Their sons and daughters sold into the hand of Judah (Joel 3:8)"),
    ("Edom", "Stood on the other side; cut off the escaped (Obadiah 11, 14)", "“As thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee” (Obadiah 15)"),
    ("The conqueror of Habakkuk 2", "Spoiled many nations (Habakkuk 2:8)", "The remnant of the people shall spoil him"),
    ("Egypt", "A staff of reed to Israel; “My river is mine own” (Ezekiel 29:3, 6)", "The basest of kingdoms; the rivers made dry (Ezekiel 29:15; 30:12)"),
  ], [0.2, 0.38, 0.42]),
("sub", "7.6 What the volume does not take up"),
("p", "The volume stops at the edge of three questions that belong elsewhere. The day of the LORD is read through judgment and restoration in Study 7, and the volume does not restate that study. The court of the living nations, the sheep and the goats of Matthew 25, is read in Prophecy & Mystery Volume 5 among the separate courts, and the volume does not set Joel 3 or Zephaniah 3:8 into that court. And the war of Ezekiel 38 and 39, with its names of nations, is not argued here. The volume’s point is smaller. It is that the oracles against the nations tie to a day, that the cup of Jeremiah 25 and the cup of Obadiah 16 and Habakkuk 2:16 are one figure, and that the grounds fit the sentences."),
("sub", "7.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The cup of Jeremiah 25 passes in order from Jerusalem to “all the kingdoms of the world,” and the king of Babylon, the LORD’s instrument, is on the list (Jeremiah 25:12, 26).",
    "Jeremiah 25:29–33 widens the sentence from the city called by the LORD’s name to all flesh; the volume holds the roar and the slain of verses 30 and 33 as open, since no later narrative reports them.",
    "Obadiah 15 states the measure as a rule, “as thou hast done, it shall be done unto thee,” and Obadiah 16, Jeremiah 25 and Habakkuk 2:16 carry one figure, a cup that is drunk.",
    "Joel 3:1, 2 ties the gathering of the nations to the time the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem is brought again, and Zephaniah 3:8 states the gathering as the LORD’s own determination.",
    "The oracles’ sentences are cut to their grounds; the volume treats this as a rule of the oracles and leaves Babylon, Edom, Study 7, the court of Matthew 25 and Ezekiel 38–39 to the places that read them.",
  ]),
]
